FC-D10 Blueprint v0.7 — Flying Carpet Delivery
Paper sizing study. No hardware, no ground test, no flight test, no prototype exists. Status convention: [V] verified by derivation or by citable external specification (literature, datasheet, regulation); [S] standard engineering practice, not independently verified for this configuration; [H] hypothesis or unmeasured quantity. No MTOW is a specification. Every numerical value is a computed or sourced estimate, not a measurement.

---

0. Scope and epistemic status

This document is a mathematical and architectural sizing study. It contains no experimental result, no bench data, no flight data, and no prototype. All quantitative claims are either (i) derived from the equations stated here, (ii) taken from a citable external specification and marked as such, or (iii) carried as an explicit hypothesis to be tested. The purpose of v0.7 is to establish a falsifiable closure procedure: a set of equations, inequalities, and architectural choices that can later be proved or disproved by measurement. The document deliberately stops short of asserting a demonstrated mass, endurance, or flight capability.

The efficiency values, battery ratings, and aerodynamic coefficients used below are literature-sourced or datasheet-sourced working values, not measurements made by this programme. They are chosen to be conservative where the source is uncertain, and they are labelled accordingly. Any value marked [H] is a placeholder that must be replaced by a measured value before an MTOW is frozen.

---

1. Aerodynamics

1.1. Glauert momentum balance with disk tilt

For a rotor whose thrust vector is tilted by an angle \chi from the vertical, the component of the free-stream velocity normal to the disk is V_c=V\sin\chi. With v_h=\sqrt{T/(2\rho A_{\rm tot})}, the physically admissible induced velocity is the positive root of

v_i^2 + V_c\,v_i - v_h^2 = 0,
\qquad
v_i = -\frac{V_c}{2} + \sqrt{\frac{V_c^2}{4}+v_h^2}.
\tag{1}

The v0.4 form carried a plus sign on the V^2 term. That is the climb form and is discarded: it corresponds to an ascending rotor, not to edgewise flight. The induced power at the disk is P_i=\kappa T v_i, with \kappa tabulated against the advance ratio \mu=V/(\Omega R):

\kappa(\mu)=
\begin{cases}
1.10, & \mu<0.05,\\
1.15, & 0.05\le\mu<0.10,\\
1.20, & 0.10\le\mu<0.20,\\
1.25, & \mu\ge0.20.
\end{cases}
\tag{2}

For \mu>0.1 the high-speed limit

P_{i,\rm fwd}\approx\frac{\kappa T^2}{2\rho A_{\rm tot}V}
\tag{3}

is retained only as a lower bound, because it neglects tip loss. Eq. (1) governs sizing; Eq. (3) is a consistency check, not a design equation.

1.2. Disk tilt as an explicit variable

A carpet with lift-to-drag ratio L/D sustains a steady tilt \chi\approx\arctan(D/L) in forward flight. The v0.7 domain therefore carries \chi as a free variable consistent with the assumed f(\theta) of the envelope, and computes V_c=V\sin\chi inside Eq. (1). The claim that a carpet at L/D\approx2\text{–}3 produces \chi\approx15^\circ\text{–}25^\circ and a 10\%\text{–}18\% induced-power increase is plausible but unverified; it is carried as [S] and must be computed from Eq. (1), not hard-coded.

1.3. Profile power

The first-order blade-element result is

P_{\rm profile}
=
\frac18\,\rho A_{\rm tot}(\Omega R)^3\,\sigma C_{d0}\,(1+4.65\mu^2),
\tag{4}

with \sigma=0.10 and C_{d0}=0.012 as working values [H]. At 30-inch rotors and V=15\ \mathrm{m/s}, Eq. (4) yields an estimate of order 0.8\text{–}1.2\ \mathrm{kW} for eight rotors, comparable to or larger than the induced power. C_{d0} is therefore the dominant cruise uncertainty and is a required measurement (Section 9). No value of C_{d0} in this document is a measurement.

---

2. Efficiency convention and a computed hover example

2.1. One convention per regime

The v0.4 and v0.5 formulations stacked a hover figure of merit \eta_P, a separate \kappa, a separate profile term, and a separate propulsive efficiency, which double-counts profile loss. v0.7 enforces a single convention per regime:

\boxed{
P_{\rm elec}=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{P_{\rm ideal,hover}}{\eta_P\,\eta_{\rm motor}\,\eta_{\rm ESC}\,\eta_{\rm wire}}, & \text{hover},\\[10pt]
\dfrac{P_i+P_{\rm profile}+P_{\rm parasite}}{\eta_{\rm prop}\,\eta_{\rm motor}\,\eta_{\rm ESC}\,\eta_{\rm wire}}, & \text{cruise}.
\end{cases}}
\tag{5}

The working values are literature-sourced, not measured by this programme:

\eta_P=0.60\ [\mathrm{S}],\quad
\eta_{\rm motor}=0.81\text{–}0.85\ [\mathrm{V}],\quad
\eta_{\rm ESC}=0.89\text{–}0.91\ [\mathrm{V}],\quad
\eta_{\rm wire}=0.98\ [\mathrm{S}],
\tag{6}

and \eta_{\rm prop}=0.60 baseline / 0.70 optimistic [H]. The motor and ESC ranges are taken from published flight-test efficiency decompositions, which report a hover motor efficiency of 81.4\% and an ESC efficiency of 88.7\%\text{–}90.9\%. The v0.6 values \eta_{\rm motor}=0.90 and \eta_{\rm ESC}=0.95 are withdrawn as optimistic; they are not supported by the published decompositions. The product \eta_P\eta_{\rm motor}\eta_{\rm ESC}\eta_{\rm wire} is not multiplied by \eta_{\rm prop}, because the two efficiencies apply at different flight states.

2.2. Computed hover example (illustrative, not measured)

At m=35\ \mathrm{kg} and A_{\rm tot}=3.65\ \mathrm{m^2}, the ideal momentum power is

P_{\rm ideal}
=
\frac{(mg)^{3/2}}{\sqrt{2\rho A_{\rm tot}}}
=
\frac{(343.2)^{3/2}}{\sqrt{2(1.225)(3.65)}}
\approx 2.13\ \mathrm{kW}.
\tag{7}

With the mid-range chain 0.60\times0.83\times0.90\times0.98=0.439, the computed battery-electrical hover power is

P_{\rm hover,elec}\approx\frac{2.13}{0.439}\approx4.85\ \mathrm{kW}.
\tag{8}

Eq. (8) is a calculation, not a measurement. It is the number that enters the power-limited battery inequality of Section 4.

