3. Mass closure

3.1. Budget

m_{\rm MTOW}
=
m_{\rm payload}+m_{\rm batt}+m_{\rm prop}+m_{\rm chassis}
+m_{\rm ring}+m_{\rm av}+m_{\rm lg}+m_{\rm env}+m_{\rm margin}.
\tag{9}

The fractions are bracketed by published UAV practice, not by this programme's measurements:

Term Fraction of MTOW Status
m_{\rm chassis} 28–32 % [S] composite multirotor structure, literature bracket
m_{\rm ring} 4–6 % additional [H] stiffness-driven carpet ring
m_{\rm lg} 5–8 % [S] UAV landing-gear practice
m_{\rm av} 5 % [S] avionics and wiring
m_{\rm env} 0.5–1.5 kg explicit [H] fabric and attachments

The landing-gear fraction is 5–8 %, not the 15–17 % carried in v0.4. The 15–17 % figure has no verified source and is withdrawn.

3.2. Proof that the v0.4 fractions cannot close

The v0.4 allocation was m_{\rm payload}/m_{\rm MTOW}=0.286, m_{\rm structure}/m_{\rm MTOW}=0.28\text{–}0.35, m_{\rm lg}/m_{\rm MTOW}\le0.15, m_{\rm margin}/m_{\rm MTOW}=0.10\text{–}0.15. Their sum is

0.286+0.28+0.10=0.666,
\qquad
0.286+0.35+0.15+0.15=0.936.
\tag{10}

The residual available for propulsion, battery, avionics and envelope is between 6.4\% and 33.4\% of MTOW. Since m_{\rm batt} alone is of order 8\text{–}21\% of MTOW in the corrected closure, and m_{\rm prop} and m_{\rm av} are non-zero, the lower v0.4 combination is arithmetically non-closing. This is a formal refutation, not a preference.

3.3. Fixed point, contraction, and post-convergence margin

Define the physical mass M as the solution of

M=\Phi(M),
\qquad
\Phi(M)=m_{\rm payload}+m_{\rm batt}(M)+m_{\rm prop}(M)+f_{\rm struct}M
+m_{\rm av}+m_{\rm lg}(M)+m_{\rm env},
\tag{11}

with f_{\rm struct}=0.32\text{–}0.38 as the initial bracket for the combined chassis-plus-ring fraction. A unique fixed point exists on any interval where \Phi is a contraction:

L
\equiv
f_{\rm struct}
+
\frac{\partial m_{\rm batt}}{\partial M}
+
\frac{\partial m_{\rm prop}}{\partial M}
<1.
\tag{12}

Because m_{\rm batt}(M) and m_{\rm prop}(M) are increasing and bounded over the operating envelope, and f_{\rm struct}<1, condition (12) is satisfied whenever the battery and propulsion mass sensitivities are moderate. It is checked numerically in the closure solver, not assumed analytically. Stage 1 iterates Eq. (11) with m_{\rm margin}=0 to obtain M^{*}. Stage 2 adds the fixed reserve:

m_{\rm margin}=0.12\,M^{*},
\qquad
m_{\rm MTOW}=1.12\,M^{*}.
\tag{13}

Both M^{*} and 1.12M^{*} are reported. A configuration that closes only when the margin is absorbed into Eq. (11) is declared non-closing.

The carbon-tube failure figure of \sim490\ \mathrm{N} at safety factor 1.85 is unverified [H]; published carbon spars span a wide range, from roughly 2\ \mathrm{kN} to 18\ \mathrm{kN} ultimate load depending on layup and geometry. The ring and arm sizing must therefore be carried by a specific bending-and-torsion calculation for the chosen tube, not by a generic benchmark.

