5. Propulsion geometry

5.1. Rotor separation

s_{cc}=D+\delta D,
\qquad
\delta\in[0.15,\;0.20],
\qquad
l=\frac{s_{cc}}{D}\in[1.8,\;2.4]\ \text{for staggered layouts}.
\tag{15}

For a 30-inch rotor (D=0.762\ \mathrm{m}) this gives s_{cc}=0.876\text{–}0.914\ \mathrm{m}. The v0.4 phrase “0.46–0.61 m beyond the radius” is withdrawn as dimensionally inconsistent with Eq. (15).

5.2. Coaxial exclusion

Simple momentum analysis gives an interference factor k_{\rm int}=\sqrt2\approx1.414 in the close-spacing limit and 1.281 at maximum separation; published experiments reduce the maximum-separation value to 1.16, yielding a practical factor of 1.280. The theoretical minimum is 1.2657, and in practice k_{\rm int}>1.2657. The validated range is

k_{\rm int}\in[1.2657,\;1.414],
\qquad
\text{practical }k_{\rm int}\approx1.28.
\tag{16}

The lower bound of 1.219 carried in the v0.7 review lies below the theoretical minimum and is rejected. Coaxial pairs are excluded from the baseline: a \ge26.6\% induced-power penalty is inconsistent with a power-limited design.

5.3. Disk loading

\mathrm{DL}=\frac{m_{\rm MTOW}\,g}{N_r\pi r_{\rm prop}^2}.
\tag{17}

Eight 30-inch rotors at 35 kg give \mathrm{DL}\approx94\ \mathrm{N/m^2}; eight 26-inch rotors at 24 kg give \mathrm{DL}\approx86\ \mathrm{N/m^2}. The design target \mathrm{DL}<150\ \mathrm{N/m^2} is satisfied by both.

---

6. Architecture and design

6.1. Two-configuration strategy

The C3 dimension constraint is a hard geometric inequality. A class C3 UAS must have MTOM including payload below 25 kg and a maximum characteristic dimension below 3 m. A 2\times4 grid of 30-inch rotors at s_{cc}\approx0.9\ \mathrm{m} has a long-axis extent of roughly 4D+3\delta D\approx3.4\text{–}3.5\ \mathrm{m}, which exceeds the limit. C3 is therefore impossible with that layout, independent of mass.

v0.7 defines two configurations:

· C3 demonstrator. Eight 26-inch rotors, staggered hexagonal layout with l=2.0, target maximum dimension below 3\ \mathrm{m} and MTOW below 25\ \mathrm{kg}. The claimed \sim2.85\ \mathrm{m} diagonal is a geometric estimate [S] and must be verified against the chosen arm lengths before the layout is frozen. No geometry has been built or measured.
· Specific-category flight article. Eight 30-inch rotors in a staggered layout, 35–45 kg class, operated under a SORA-based authorisation.

Both configurations share the chassis, ring, and avionics architecture; only the propulsion arms and rotor set change. Neither configuration exists as hardware at the time of writing.

6.2. Structure

The structural architecture is a carbon-composite central torsion box carrying the payload platform, surrounded by a peripheral ring that defines the carpet silhouette and carries three skids. Three skids avoid over-constraining the flexible fabric and produce deterministic load paths [S]. The ring is bending-and-torsion dominated and scales differently from the chassis; it is carried as a separate mass term and is sized by a specific beam calculation, not by a generic fraction. The beam calculation is a design requirement, not a completed analysis.

6.3. Envelope

The fabric envelope is non-load-bearing, permeable over the rotor disks through a structural grille, and kept entirely out of the rotor inflow. The envelope mass is carried explicitly in Eq. (9). The carpet-as-lifting-surface hypothesis — qS\approx0.9\ \mathrm{kN} against W\approx0.34\ \mathrm{kN} at 15\ \mathrm{m/s} for S\approx6.5\ \mathrm{m^2} — remains [H] and is not admitted to the baseline closure. It is a candidate mechanism to be tested, not a load-bearing assumption.

6.4. Avionics and safety

Redundant flight controller, RTK GNSS, IMU, barometer, downward LiDAR for landing, and a ballistic parachute for cruise-phase failures. Eight rotors do not guarantee continued flight after a single motor failure. Motor-out capability is a design requirement to be demonstrated by thrust-allocation simulation before flight; the 60 % throttle-at-hover rule is retained only as a sizing guideline [S], not as a proof of redundancy.

