8. Feasibility domain and hard screens

The domain is \{A_{\rm tot},\chi,f(\theta),e_{\rm pack},V,d\}, with inequality screens replacing the adjectives of v0.4:

1. \mathrm{DL}<150\ \mathrm{N/m^2};
2. Eq. (14) satisfied with the datasheet 3C continuous rating;
3. m_{\rm batt}\ge3.0\ \mathrm{kg} as a thermal-management floor [S];
4. the Stage-1 fixed point exists and satisfies the contraction condition (12);
5. m_{\rm MTOW}<50\ \mathrm{kg} on the Specific article, and m_{\rm MTOW}<25\ \mathrm{kg} with L_{\max}<3\ \mathrm{m} on the C3 demonstrator.

The domain is a computational exercise. It produces four outcome classes: A. physical closure satisfying all screens; B. numerical closure but violation of a screen (typically the 3C continuous rating); C. non-closure within the domain; D. sensitivity dominance by f(\theta) and the empty-mass fraction, which identifies the first quantities that a future experimental programme must measure.

---

9. Required validation gates (not executed)

The following five measurements are required before any MTOW is frozen. None has been performed. They are listed here as the experimental programme that the mathematics demands, not as results:

1. Thrust-stand figure-of-merit and C_{d0} test — required to anchor Eq. (5) and replace the working C_{d0} with a measured value.
2. 3C continuous-discharge thermal test at 45\ ^\circ\mathrm{C} ambient for 10 min — required to fix k_{\rm derate} and verify whether the datasheet 3C continuous rating is sustained inside the ring enclosure.
3. Drag-versus-pitch test on a carpet mock-up — required to measure f(\theta) and hence the tilt \chi that enters Eq. (1).
4. Static load test at 1.65\,T/W on the chassis–ring–skid assembly — required to replace the unverified 490 N carbon-tube benchmark with a specific tube calculation and a specific test.
5. Monte Carlo sampling of f_{\rm struct}, e_{\rm pack}, C_{d0} — required to convert the deterministic fixed point into a probability of contraction of Eq. (12); this is a computational gate, not an experiment, and it can be executed on the existing model without hardware.

Until these gates are passed, the FC-D10 has a falsifiable closure procedure but no demonstrated mass, endurance, or flight capability.

