Appendix B — Aerodynamic derivations

B.1. Glauert quadratic and the sign proof

The axial momentum balance for a rotor with disk-normal inflow V_c and induced velocity v_i is

T = 2\rho A_{\rm tot}\,(V_c+v_i)\,v_i .
\tag{B.1}

Dividing by 2\rho A_{\rm tot} and using v_h^2=T/(2\rho A_{\rm tot}):

v_h^2 = (V_c+v_i)v_i = V_c v_i + v_i^2 .
\tag{B.2}

Rearranging into standard quadratic form:

v_i^2 + V_c\,v_i - v_h^2 = 0 .
\tag{B.3}

The roots are

v_i^{(\pm)} = \frac{-V_c \pm \sqrt{V_c^2+4v_h^2}}{2}.
\tag{B.4}

The physically admissible root is the one with v_i>0, hence

\boxed{
v_i = -\frac{V_c}{2}+\sqrt{\frac{V_c^2}{4}+v_h^2}
}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
v_i^{(-)}<0 \ \text{is rejected}.
\tag{B.5}

Structural statement. The linear coefficient of Eq. (B.3) is negative. The v0.4 formulation, written as v_i^2 - V v_i - v_h^2 = 0 after the sign error, corresponds to an ascending rotor (climb form), not to edgewise flight. The error was not numerical; it was a misidentification of the flight state.

Limit checks.

V_c\to 0:\quad v_i\to v_h
\qquad
V_c\gg v_h:\quad v_i\to \frac{v_h^2}{V_c},
\tag{B.6}

the second being the familiar high-speed decay of induced velocity and the origin of the 1/V induced-power asymptote.

B.2. Disk tilt coupling

In steady forward flight at speed V, the thrust vector must balance weight and drag simultaneously. Let \chi be the tilt of the thrust axis from the vertical. Force balance gives

T\cos\chi = W,
\qquad
T\sin\chi = D_{\rm total},
\qquad
\tan\chi = \frac{D_{\rm total}}{W}.
\tag{B.7}

The disk-normal inflow is therefore

V_c = V\sin\chi .
\tag{B.8}

Two consequences follow. First, the induced velocity is lower than the untilted case at the same T, because Eq. (B.5) is monotone decreasing in V_c. Second, the thrust magnitude must increase by 1/\cos\chi to carry the same weight, which raises the induced power through the T v_i product. The net effect is

P_i = \kappa T v_i
=
\kappa\,\frac{W}{\cos\chi}\,
\left[-\frac{V\sin\chi}{2}+\sqrt{\frac{V^2\sin^2\chi}{4}+v_h^2(\chi)}\right],
\qquad
v_h^2(\chi)=\frac{W}{2\rho A_{\rm tot}\cos\chi}.
\tag{B.9}

Eq. (B.9) makes \chi an explicit variable. The claim that a carpet at L/D\approx2\text{–}3 produces \chi\approx15^\circ\text{–}25^\circ and a 10\%\text{–}18\% induced-power penalty is not derived here and is not claimed; it is a hypothesis to be evaluated by substituting the measured f(\theta) into Eq. (B.7).

B.3. Parasite power

P_{\rm parasite} = \frac{1}{2}\rho f V^3 ,
\tag{B.10}

where f=f(\theta_{\rm pitch}) is the equivalent flat-plate area. At V=15\ \mathrm{m/s}, f=1.0\ \mathrm{m^2}:

P_{\rm parasite} = 0.5(1.225)(1.0)(3375) \approx 2.07\ \mathrm{kW}.
\tag{B.11}

This term alone exceeds the untilted induced power at 35 kg, which is why f is a first-order parameter and why treating it as a constant is inadmissible for a carpet.

B.4. Profile power, first-order BEMT

The minimum profile-power coefficient is

C_{P0} = \frac{\sigma C_{d0}}{8},
\tag{B.12}

giving

P_{\rm profile}
=
\frac18\rho A_{\rm tot}(\Omega R)^3\sigma C_{d0}(1+4.65\mu^2).
\tag{B.13}

The correction factor (1+4.65\mu^2) accounts for the rise in mean profile drag with advance ratio. Both \sigma and C_{d0} are [H]; C_{d0} is Reynolds-dependent and the dominant cruise uncertainty.

B.5. Total cruise electrical power

P_{\rm cruise,elec}
=
\frac{P_i + P_{\rm profile} + P_{\rm parasite}}
{\eta_{\rm prop}\,\eta_{\rm motor}\,\eta_{\rm ESC}\,\eta_{\rm wire}}.
\tag{B.14}

B.6. Coaxial interference

For two coaxial rotors of equal disk area sharing total thrust T, ideal momentum theory with vanishing separation gives each rotor an induced velocity equal to that of an isolated rotor carrying the full thrust, hence

k_{\rm int,ideal} = \sqrt{2} \approx 1.4142 .
\tag{B.15}

At maximum separation the ideal value drops to 1.281; published experiments reduce the maximum-separation figure to 1.16, implying a practical factor of 1.280. The theoretical minimum is 1.2657, and in practice k_{\rm int}>1.2657. The accepted bracket is

k_{\rm int}\in[1.2657,\;1.414],
\qquad
k_{\rm int,practical}\approx1.28 .
\tag{B.16}

A value of 1.219 lies below the theoretical minimum and is rejected. The corresponding induced-power penalty of at least 26.57\% is incompatible with a power-limited design, which is the formal basis for excluding coaxial pairs.

