The v0.4 landing-gear allocation of \le15\% is withdrawn; the 15–17 % figure has no verified source. The accepted bracket is 5–8 %, consistent with published UAV practice.

D.2. Formal non-closure proof for the v0.4 allocation

v0.4 used p=0.286, s\in[0.28,0.35], g\le0.15, \mu\in[0.10,0.15]. The lower sum is

S_{\min} = 0.286+0.28+0.10 = 0.666,
\tag{D.2}

and the upper sum is

S_{\max} = 0.286+0.35+0.15+0.15 = 0.936 .
\tag{D.3}

The residual available to propulsion, battery, avionics, and envelope is 1-S, i.e. 0.064 to 0.334. Since the corrected closure places m_{\rm batt} alone at 0.08\text{–}0.21 of MTOW, and m_{\rm prop}+m_{\rm av}>0, there is an interval of the v0.4 parameter space, including the entire lower bracket, for which

\sum_i \frac{m_i}{m_{\rm MTOW}} > 1 .
\tag{D.4}

A configuration satisfying Eq. (D.4) has no positive solution. The v0.4 allocation is therefore arithmetically non-closing on a non-empty subset of its own stated range — a formal refutation, not a judgement.

D.3. Fixed point and contraction condition

Define

\Phi(M) = m_{\rm payload} + m_{\rm batt}(M) + m_{\rm prop}(M)
+ (f_c+f_r+f_a+f_g)M + m_{\rm env},
\tag{D.5}

and let f_{\rm struct}=f_c+f_r. Stage 1 solves M=\Phi(M). By the Banach fixed-point theorem, a unique solution exists in a closed interval I if \Phi:I\to I and \Phi is a contraction there:

\left|\Phi'(M)\right| \le L < 1,
\qquad
L = f_{\rm struct} + \frac{\partial m_{\rm batt}}{\partial M} + \frac{\partial m_{\rm prop}}{\partial M} .
\tag{D.6}

D.4. Explicit sensitivity derivatives

Power-limited battery branch. Since P_{\rm hover,elec}\propto M^{3/2} and m_{\rm batt}^{\rm power}=P_{\rm hover,elec}/({\rm C}\,e_{\rm pack}),

m_{\rm batt}^{\rm power} = c_p M^{3/2}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\frac{\partial m_{\rm batt}^{\rm power}}{\partial M}
=
\frac{3}{2}\,\frac{m_{\rm batt}^{\rm power}}{M}.
\tag{D.7}

Energy-limited battery branch. If m_{\rm batt}^{\rm energy}=c_e M^{\alpha} with \alpha\in[1,1.5] depending on the hover/cruise energy split,

\frac{\partial m_{\rm batt}^{\rm energy}}{\partial M}
=
\alpha\,\frac{m_{\rm batt}^{\rm energy}}{M}.
\tag{D.8}

Propulsion, constant disk loading. If r_{\rm prop} is chosen to hold DL fixed, r_{\rm prop}^2\propto M, so m_{\rm prop}\propto M and

\frac{\partial m_{\rm prop}}{\partial M} = \frac{m_{\rm prop}}{M}.
\tag{D.9}

If r_{\rm prop} is fixed by the selected rotor, m_{\rm prop} is constant and this derivative vanishes.

Numerical evaluation at 35 kg. With m_{\rm batt}/M\approx0.13, m_{\rm prop}/M\approx0.15, and the power branch active (\alpha=1.5):

L \approx 0.38 + 1.5(0.13) + 0.15 = 0.725 .
\tag{D.10}

At the upper structural bracket the margin to L=1 is 0.275. If the energy branch is active with \alpha=1 and m_{\rm batt}/M=0.21, then

L \approx 0.38 + 0.21 + 0.15 = 0.74 .
\tag{D.11}

The contraction condition is satisfied across the plausible bracket, but not with a wide margin. This is a structural property of the problem and is the mathematical reason why the Monte Carlo gate of Appendix L is required: a small upward perturbation of f_{\rm struct} and m_{\rm prop} can push L toward 1 and destroy uniqueness.

D.5. Margin protocol

Stage 1 yields M^{*}. Stage 2 applies

m_{\rm margin} = 0.12\,M^{*},
\qquad
m_{\rm MTOW}=1.12\,M^{*}.
\tag{D.12}

Reporting both numbers separately is mandatory. A configuration whose closure depends on m_{\rm margin}\neq0 is declared non-closing.

