Appendix E — Battery model, crossover, and dual-pack logic

E.1. Power-limited sizing

m_{\rm batt}
=
\max\left(
\underbrace{\frac{E_{\rm mission}/{\rm DoD}}{k_{\rm derate}\,e_{\rm pack}}}_{m_E},
\;
\underbrace{\frac{P_{\rm hover,elec}}{{\rm C}_{\rm cont}\,e_{\rm pack}}}_{m_P}
\right).
\tag{E.1}

E.2. Crossover derivation

The crossover mass M_X is defined by m_E(M_X)=m_P(M_X). With E_{\rm mission}\propto M^{\alpha} and P_{\rm hover,elec}\propto M^{3/2},

\frac{c_e M_X^{\alpha}}{{\rm DoD}\,k_{\rm derate}\,e_{\rm pack}}
=
\frac{c_p M_X^{3/2}}{{\rm C}_{\rm cont}\,e_{\rm pack}}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
M_X^{3/2-\alpha}
=
\frac{c_e\,{\rm C}_{\rm cont}}{c_p\,{\rm DoD}\,k_{\rm derate}} .
\tag{E.2}

For a hover-dominated mission (\alpha=3/2), Eq. (E.2) has no solution in M: the two branches scale identically and one dominates everywhere. For a parasite-dominated cruise mission (\alpha=1),

M_X^{1/2} = \frac{c_e\,{\rm C}_{\rm cont}}{c_p\,{\rm DoD}\,k_{\rm derate}}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
M_X = \left(\frac{c_e\,{\rm C}_{\rm cont}}{c_p\,{\rm DoD}\,k_{\rm derate}}\right)^{2}.
\tag{E.3}

The crossover is therefore mission-dependent, not a fixed number. Above M_X the design is power-limited; below it, energy-limited. The claim of a crossover near 38 kg is consistent with an \alpha\approx1 cruise-dominated mission and the numerical values of Appendix C, but it is a computed result of the present parameter set, not a universal constant.

E.3. Datasheet ratings

